    {
      "field_name": "diagnostic_criteria",
      "value": "clinical features such as trunk and limb rigidity, muscle spasms, brainstem dysfunction, and autonomic instability; positive anti-GlyRα1 antibodies in serum and CSF; CSF pleocytosis and elevated IgG index; oligoclonal bands; response to immunotherapy with stabilization",
      "quote": "Progressive encephalomyelitis with rigidity and myoclonus (PERM) is an inflammatory disorder of the stiff-person syndrome spectrum that presents subacutely with trunk and limb rigidity, muscle spasms, brainstem dysfunction, and autonomic instability [1, 2]. PERM has been strongly associated with IgG1 and IgG3 autoantibodies against the glycine receptor alpha-1 subunit (GlyRα1)[3, 4]. Repeat CSF studies showed pleocytosis (11 nucleated cells, 98% lymphocytes), normal protein, elevated Ig G index, and central nervous system (CNS)-specific oligoclonal bands. Neuronal autoantibodies were sent from serum and CSF and both were strongly positive for anti-GlyRα1 antibodies (Mayo Clinic Laboratories).",
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "field_name": "diagnostic_basis_clinical",
      "value": "used",
      "quote": "presents subacutely with trunk and limb rigidity, muscle spasms, brainstem dysfunction, and autonomic instability",
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
