      "value": "used",
      "quote": "strongly associated with IgG1 and IgG3 autoantibodies against the glycine receptor alpha-1 subunit (GlyRα1)[3, 4]. Neuronal autoantibodies were sent from serum and CSF and both were strongly positive for anti-GlyRα1 antibodies (Mayo Clinic Laboratories).",
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "field_name": "diagnostic_basis_emg",
      "value": "N/A",
      "quote": "N/A",
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "field_name": "diagnostic_basis_treatment_response",
      "value": "used",
      "quote": "Immunotherapy was empirically started with high dose steroids and therapeutic plasmapheresis. Despite incomplete plasmapheresis sessions due to autonomic instability, the patient's clinical condition stabilized.",
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "field_name": "diagnostic_basis_imaging",
      "value": "used",
      "quote": "Magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) of the brain, performed two days after lumbar puncture (LP), revealed mild diffuse pachymeningeal enhancement, bilateral optic nerve enhancement and a prominent left anterior cavernous sinus (Fig. 1).",
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "field_name": "diagnostic_basis_exclusion_of_mimics",
      "value": "used",
      "quote": "Other autoantibodies, including glutamic acid decarboxylase-65 antibodies, N-methyl-D-aspartate receptor antibodies, anti-neutrophil cytoplasmic antibodies, and paraneoplastic panel (Mayo Clinic Laboratories) were negative, immunoglobulin IgG4 was within normal limits and QuantiFERON was indeterminate. Occult malignancy was not identified on computed tomography (CT) of the chest, abdomen or pelvis as well as scrotal and renal ultrasounds.",
      "confidence": "high"
    },
